<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Segeda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Segeda was a Celtiberian city of the Belli in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, near modern Mara in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>. When it enlarged its walls and drew in the neighbouring Titti in 154 BCE, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> treated this as a breach of treaty and declared war. The urgency of that campaign led Rome to move the start of the consular year to 1 January in 153 BCE, and the city was abandoned soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/segeda.jpg" alt="Segeda" width="320" height="130" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zona_arqueol%C3%B3gica_de_Segeda,_Belmonte_de_Graci%C3%A1n,_Zaragoza,_Espa%C3%B1a,_2017-01-05,_DD_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
